# Normalize to LF in the repository. Studio is built on Windows (Lazarus/Delphi) and its
# CI runs on Linux; Pascal sources travel between the two, and a CRLF round-trip through
# git would show every line as changed.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must stay LF even on a Windows checkout: CRLF breaks the shebang.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Pascal sources
*.pas text eol=lf
*.lpr text eol=lf
*.lfm text eol=lf
*.inc text eol=lf

# Prompt fixtures: NO normalisation of any kind, for the whole directory.
#
# These files are compared BYTE FOR BYTE against src/SpxPrompt.pas, so whatever git changes on
# the way in or out is a difference the port gets blamed for. This rule has now been written
# twice, and the first version was too clever: it normalised most of the directory to LF and
# exempted only `clean-*`, on the reasoning that those were the cases that might hold a CR. A
# week's worth of that reasoning lasted one commit -- the next fixture to carry CRLF was
# `repair-crlf`, a `.template.txt` and a `.user.txt`, and both were silently rewritten. Twice
# in one session, the same way: one warning line in the middle of a large add, and a working
# tree that still held the original so the suite stayed green.
#
# So: no exceptions, no per-extension reasoning, nothing to keep in step with what the corpus
# happens to contain. These are machine-generated and machine-compared. Read them through
# scripts/dump-prompt-fixtures.mjs, not through a diff.
tests/fixtures/prompt-v2/** -text

# Binary assets, never diffed or line-normalized
*.ico binary
*.png binary
*.res binary
